# Scripts that run inside Linux containers must keep LF regardless of the
# checkout platform — a CRLF-terminated `sh` script fails with obscure
# "unexpected word" syntax errors, since the stray \r becomes part of a token.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Manifests under deploy/ are generated with LF by
# scripts/secrets/New-ExternalSecrets.ps1 and compared against the checkout,
# byte for byte, by ci.yml. Without this, a checkout on the Windows box turns
# every generated file into drift the moment core.autocrlf touches it.
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# necessary for standard line endings for some reason
*.cs    text
*.json  text
*.sln   text
*.csproj    text

# PowerShell, for the same reason and found the same way. Every remote command
# in scripts/ is a string sent to a Linux node over SSH, and a here-string in a
# CRLF checkout carries \r into the last token of each of its lines: a
# `df ... | tail -n 1` arrived at a node as `tail -n 1\r` and was answered with
# `tail: invalid number of lines: '1\r'`, which reads as a broken node and is
# a broken checkout. Invoke-NodeSsh normalises it now as well - this is the
# other end of the same fix, so that the bytes never arrive wrong rather than
# only being corrected on the way out.
*.ps1   text eol=lf
